{% extends "page.j2" %}
{% from "icons.j2" import link_arrow %}
{% set active_page = 'about' %}
{% set page_title = "About the contest" %}
{% set page_desc = "Rules, scoring, and divisions for NC(J)MO." %}
{% block content %}
    <nav class="page-links" aria-label="On this page"><a class="text-link" href="#rules-scoring">Rules &amp; scoring</a><a class="text-link" href="#divisions">Divisions</a><a class="text-link" href="#common-questions">Questions</a></nav>
    <section class="section-space rules-section" id="rules-scoring" aria-labelledby="rules-title">
        <div class="prose"><h2 class="section-title" id="rules-title">Rules &amp; scoring</h2><p id="contest-format">NC(J)MO follows a BAMO-style proof-based format.</p></div>
        <div class="rules-layout">
            <div class="format-panel">
                <h3>Contest format</h3>
                <table>
                    <caption>Both NCJMO and NCMO</caption>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Problems</th><td>5 proof problems</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Time limit</th><td>3 hours</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Per problem</th><td>Up to 7 points</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Total</th><td>Up to 35 points</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="rules-explanation">
                <h3>Scoring</h3>
                <p>Solutions are graded on completeness, clarity, and correctness. A complete solution includes the reasoning that proves your answer.</p>
                <p>Useful progress can earn partial credit. Submit the work you have, even if you have not fully solved a problem.</p>
                <h3>Permitted materials</h3>
                <p>The published contest instructions permit only writing utensils, erasers, and an optional straightedge and compass as tools.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="solution-instructions">
            <h3>Writing and submitting solutions</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>Explain your reasoning clearly, with enough detail to justify your conclusions.</li>
                <li>When a problem asks for the greatest or least possible value, give an example that achieves your answer and prove that no better value is possible.</li>
                <li>Label each page as your contest paper instructs. The 2025 papers ask for your contestant ID, problem number, and solution page number in the top right corner of every page you submit.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <p class="rules-source">These format, scoring, and page-labeling instructions are published in the <a href="/static/2025/NCJMO-2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-describedby="new-tab-note">2025 NCJMO paper</a> and <a href="/static/2025/NCMO-2025.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-describedby="new-tab-note">2025 NCMO paper</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="two-column section-space" aria-labelledby="our-story">
        <div class="prose">
            <h2 class="section-title" id="our-story">About NC(J)MO</h2>
            <p>
                The North Carolina (Junior) Math Olympiad is a proof-based math competition.
                Through NCJMO and NCMO, students with a range of experience can work on challenging
                problems and write complete mathematical arguments.
            </p>
            <p>
                Our mission is to make proof-based olympiads accessible to the community.
                We encourage students who are new to proof writing to participate, while offering
                difficult problems that challenge experienced contestants.
            </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="info-panel" aria-labelledby="contest-practice">
            <h2 class="section-title" id="contest-practice">Past problems</h2>
            <p>Browse both divisions’ tests, solutions, and results to see the level and style of the questions.</p>
            <a class="text-link" href="/archive">Browse the archive {{ link_arrow() }}</a>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section-space" id="divisions" aria-labelledby="choose-division">
        <div class="prose">
            <h2 class="section-title" id="choose-division">Contest divisions</h2>
            <p>Compare the guidance below and explore <a href="/archive">past problems</a> to find the appropriate difficulty.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="division-grid">
            <article class="division-card">
                <h3>NCJMO</h3>
                <p>
                    Recommended for students beginning to explore math beyond school or the AMCs,
                    including those who are new to proof writing.
                </p>
                <p>The difficulty is similar to BAMO-8. Explore past NCJMO problems to see what to expect.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="division-card">
                <h3>NCMO</h3>
                <p>
                    Recommended for students who have comfortably qualified for the AIME
                    or have experience with proof writing, such as from an advanced math course.
                </p>
                <p>The difficulty is approximately USAJMO level, with a couple of easier problems at the beginning and potentially a particularly challenging one at the end.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-space prose" aria-labelledby="common-questions">
        <h2 class="section-title" id="common-questions">Common questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
            <details>
                <summary>Proofs seem hard! Should I take the NC(J)MO?</summary>
                <p>
                    First things first, don’t be intimidated by the word “proof”!
                    Think of it as a fancy way of saying, “explain your thought process as clearly as you can.”
                    We encourage you to take the NC(J)MO even if you’re inexperienced!
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does NC(J)MO have the same difficulty as USA(J)MO?</summary>
                <p>
                    NCJMO is similar in difficulty to BAMO-8. NCMO is approximately USAJMO level,
                    with a couple of easier problems at the beginning and potentially a particularly challenging one at the end.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
